// ============================================================================
// FRAMEWORK-BEHAVIOR · DISPATCH  (the hop no call site expresses)
//
// ── WHY THIS IS ITS OWN PHASE ───────────────────────────────────────────────
// The counterpart of java/engine/framework-behavior/, and it inherits that folder's
// contract verbatim:
//
//   - a rule here models a LIBRARY'S BEHAVIOUR, not the language, so no interpreter
//     can adjudicate it. CPython cannot be asked whether `send_report.delay(k)` runs
//     `send_report`; only a running worker can. Every framework NAME a rule keys on
//     therefore lives in config-resolution/knobs.dl and nowhere else, and NO RULE IN
//     THIS FILE MENTIONS A FRAMEWORK BY NAME;
//   - a rule here may only ADD relations. It must never write call_chain_edge or any
//     relation the resolution phases own. A framework model is a weaker kind of
//     knowledge than a resolved call, and folding the two together would silently
//     restate every existing consumer in terms of it;
//   - it is included AFTER call-edge-generation because it CONSUMES the resolved graph.
//
// ── WHAT IS MISSING WITHOUT IT ──────────────────────────────────────────────
// Python had exactly one framework rule: config-resolution/entry-points.dl marks an
// HTTP route handler as an entry point. Six mechanisms in wide use connect two client
// declarations with no call site between them, and every rule in call-edge-generation
// is silent across each one:
//
//   the producer writes              the consumer is                    joined by
//   send_report.delay(k)             @shared_task def send_report       a proxy object
//   def test_total(cart)             @fixture def cart                  a PARAMETER NAME
//   path("items/<k>/", detail)       def detail(request, k)             a table entry
//   order_placed.send(sender=o)      @receiver(order_placed) def on_..  a SIGNAL OBJECT
//   svc = Depends(get_service)       def get_service()                  a default value
//   @listens_for(User, "before_..")  the decorated function             a registration
//
// So `impact send_report` answers "nothing the graph can see" for a body that 493 call
// sites dispatch on one public project, and test-impact cannot reach a test through the
// fixture it injects. That is the same shape #778 reported for routes.
//
// ── WHAT IT EMITS, AND WHY NOT AS A CALL ────────────────────────────────────
//   framework_edge(FromMethod, ToMethod, Mechanism, Detail, Confidence)
//     Mechanism   task_dispatch | fixture_injection | url_dispatch |
//                 signal_dispatch | di_provider | model_hook | command_dispatch
//     Detail      what joined the two ends: the dispatch method, the parameter name,
//                 the route table, the signal binding, the marker, the hook target
//     Confidence  registered   both ends carry the framework's own registration
//                 by_name      the join is a NAME the framework resolves at run time
//                              (a fixture parameter), so it is correct exactly when
//                              the runner's scoping agrees with lexical scope
//
// A framework hop is NOT an invocation: it may cross a process (a task body runs in a
// worker), it may be asynchronous, and it carries different confidence. Emitting it as
// call_chain_edge would change the meaning of every existing consumer, so it is a
// separate relation that a consumer opts into, exactly as Java's remote_edge is.
//
// A declaration the framework calls from OUTSIDE the client also gets an entry_point
// row, which is a different claim: "this runs, with no caller here" rather than "these
// two are connected".
// ============================================================================

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 0. MATCHING A DECORATOR BY ITS TAIL
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Every catalogue in knobs.dl is a TAIL, because the same registration is written with
// and without a receiver: `@shared_task` and `@app.task(...)` and `@queue.task` are one
// mechanism. Souffle has no rfind, so the tail is TESTED rather than extracted, the way
// config-resolution/entry-points.dl tests a route verb.
//
// The receiver half is deliberately unconstrained: the author names it (app, queue,
// worker, event, v1) and pinning that list would miss the next spelling. What keeps the
// match honest is never the name alone -- it is the shape each rule requires around it.
.decl py_fw_deco_tail(v:symbol)
py_fw_deco_tail(v) :- py_task_register_deco(v).
py_fw_deco_tail(v) :- py_fixture_deco(v).
py_fw_deco_tail(v) :- py_parametrize_deco(v).
py_fw_deco_tail(v) :- py_signal_receiver_deco(v).
py_fw_deco_tail(v) :- py_orm_hook_deco(v).
py_fw_deco_tail(v) :- py_app_hook_deco(v).

// py_deco_named(DecoratorHash, Tail) -- the decorator's dotted path IS the tail, or
// ends with "." ++ tail. A bare `@task` and `@app.task` both match; `@mytask` does not,
// because a tail must be a whole dotted segment.
.decl py_deco_named(h:symbol, v:symbol)
py_deco_named(h, v) :- decorator_text("client", dp, _, _, h), py_fw_deco_tail(v), dp = v.
py_deco_named(h, v) :- decorator_text("client", dp, _, _, h), py_fw_deco_tail(v),
    strlen(dp) > strlen(v) + 1,
    substr(dp, strlen(dp) - strlen(v), strlen(v)) = v,
    substr(dp, strlen(dp) - strlen(v) - 1, 1) = ".".

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 1. TASK DISPATCH  (producer -> worker)
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// A registration decorator REPLACES the decorated name with a proxy. That is why the
// body is unreachable and also why it is findable: the producer's receiver is a binding
// whose `def` was replaced, and binding_binds_replaced_method holds exactly that def.
.decl py_task_registered(m:symbol)
py_task_registered(m) :- decorator_target("client", _, m, h), m != "",
    py_deco_named(h, v), py_task_register_deco(v).

// The worker invokes the body; nothing in the client does.
entry_point(m, "task") :- py_task_registered(m).

// `<name>.delay(...)` / `<name>.apply_async(...)` where <name> is that replaced def.
//
// THREE CONDITIONS, each load-bearing:
//   a catalogued dispatch method   `delay` alone is an ordinary method name
//   a NAME receiver                 the proxy is reached through a binding, not an
//                                   arbitrary expression
//   the binding's def is REGISTERED this is the whole test; an undecorated def, or a
//                                   parameter, produces nothing
.decl py_task_dispatch(site:symbol, from:symbol, to:symbol, meth:symbol)
py_task_dispatch(site, from, to, meth) :-
    call_decl("client", _, meth, _, _, site), py_task_dispatch_method(meth),
    call_receiver("client", "NAME", _, rexpr, site),
    call_context("client", _, from, _, _, site), from != "",
    expr_binding("client", b, ctx, rexpr), ctx != "STORE",
    binding_lookup("client", b, b2),
    binding_binds_replaced_method("client", b2, to),
    py_task_registered(to).

// (b) THE PRODUCER IMPORTED THE TASK, which is what real code does. A producer and the
// task it enqueues almost never share a module: the task lives in a tasks module and
// the caller writes `from tasks import send_report` or `import tasks`. Clause (a) alone
// resolves only the same-module case, which is the shape a single-file fixture has and
// almost nothing else. MEASURED on the task queue's own source before this clause
// existed: 20 dispatches joined against 455 declined.
py_task_dispatch(site, from, to, meth) :-
    call_decl("client", _, meth, _, _, site), py_task_dispatch_method(meth),
    call_receiver("client", "NAME", _, rexpr, site),
    call_context("client", _, from, _, _, site), from != "",
    expr_binding("client", b, ctx, rexpr), ctx != "STORE",
    binding_lookup("client", b, b2),
    import_binds_method("client", b2, to),
    py_task_registered(to).

// (c) … and the module-qualified spelling, `tasks.send_report.delay(k)`, whose receiver
// is an attribute expression rather than a bare name.
py_task_dispatch(site, from, to, meth) :-
    call_decl("client", _, meth, _, _, site), py_task_dispatch_method(meth),
    call_receiver("client", "ATTRIBUTE", _, rexpr, site),
    call_context("client", _, from, _, _, site), from != "",
    expr_attr_names_method("client", rexpr, to),
    py_task_registered(to).

framework_edge(from, to, "task_dispatch", meth, "registered") :-
    py_task_dispatch(_, from, to, meth).

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 2. FIXTURE INJECTION  (test -> fixture)
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The runner matches a test's PARAMETER NAME against the declared fixtures and calls
// the fixture to build the argument. The name is the entire join, so the confidence is
// `by_name`: it is correct exactly where the runner's scoping agrees with lexical scope.
//
// THE NAME IS THE ONE THE DECORATOR REGISTERS. `@fixture(name="client")` on
// `def client_fixture()` registers `client`, and only a parameter called `client` gets
// it. Keying on the def name wires the wrong parameter and misses the right one.
.decl py_fixture_decl(m:symbol, name:symbol)
.decl py_fixture_deco_on(m:symbol, h:symbol)
py_fixture_deco_on(m, h) :- decorator_target("client", _, m, h), m != "",
    py_deco_named(h, v), py_fixture_deco(v).
.decl py_fixture_renamed(h:symbol, name:symbol)
py_fixture_renamed(h, name) :- py_fixture_deco_on(_, h),
    annotation_arg("client", k, name, "STRING_LITERAL", _, "true", h, _),
    py_fixture_name_kw(k), name != "".
py_fixture_decl(m, name) :- py_fixture_deco_on(m, h), py_fixture_renamed(h, name).
py_fixture_decl(m, name) :- py_fixture_deco_on(m, h), !py_fixture_renamed(h, _),
    method_decl("client", name, _, _, _, m).

// The runner calls a requested fixture; nothing in the client does.
entry_point(m, "fixture") :- py_fixture_decl(m, _), py_fixture_requested(m).

// A collected test: the runner recognises it by the name it was given.
.decl py_collected_test(m:symbol)
py_collected_test(m) :- method_decl("client", name, _, _, _, m), py_test_name_prefix(p),
    strlen(name) >= strlen(p), substr(name, 0, strlen(p)) = p.

// A REQUESTER is a test, or a fixture -- a fixture may request another, and the chain
// has to carry or `impact` stops one hop short.
.decl py_fixture_requester(m:symbol)
py_fixture_requester(m) :- py_collected_test(m).
py_fixture_requester(m) :- py_fixture_decl(m, _).

// THE NEGATIVE CONDITIONS, and each one is what stops a name coincidence:
//   the owner must be a requester   an ordinary helper taking `cart` is not a test
//   the parameter must have NO default  a defaulted parameter is supplied by the
//                                   caller; the runner injects nothing
//   the parameter must not be a receiver  `self` is not a fixture request
//   some fixture must declare the name  an unmatched parameter claims nothing
// ── VISIBILITY AND SHADOWING, neither of which is optional ──────────────────
// A fixture is resolved at the test's LOCATION, not globally, and exactly ONE wins.
// The runner looks in the test's own module first; failing that, in a conftest in its
// directory and then each ancestor directory, NEAREST first. It does not run the
// others. So emitting every same-named candidate is not a conservative union over
// things that might happen, it is a claim that a function runs which demonstrably does
// not -- the same fabrication measured at 114 of 601 edges on a public subject before
// this rule existed (see knobs.dl), and the shape resolution/value-flow.dl refuses when
// it declines "every class with a method called draw".
//
// py_fixture_conftest(Fixture, Dir) -- the directory a conftest governs.
// d KEEPS ITS TRAILING SEPARATOR, and that is load-bearing rather than untidy: for
// "tests/conftest.py" it is "tests/", so "tests_extra/foo.py" does not match, because it
// starts with "tests_". Strip the separator to tidy the string and every sibling
// directory sharing a prefix becomes governed by the wrong conftest.
//
// The comparison is >=, NOT >. A repo-root conftest.py has path == basename, so a
// strict > excludes it and the commonest place a project keeps shared fixtures
// contributes nothing at all. At the root d is "" and the prefix test admits every
// requester, which is exactly right.
//
// A FIXTURE BELONGS TO EVERY MODULE IT IS AN ATTRIBUTE OF, not only to the file that
// declares it. The runner registers the fixtures it finds among a module's attributes,
// so `from .fixtures import *` in a conftest, or `from .helpers import helper_fx` in a
// test module, makes the fixture that module's own. Keyed on the declaring file alone,
// a fixture module a conftest star-imports reached no test at all (#1537).
// module_member_method already applies the language's export rule to a star import
// (`__all__`, else no leading underscore), so a name the star does not bind is not
// registered here either.
.decl py_fixture_in_file(fix:symbol, file:symbol)
py_fixture_in_file(fix, p) :- py_fixture_decl(fix, _), method_file("client", p, fix).
py_fixture_in_file(fix, p) :- py_fixture_decl(fix, _),
    module_member_method("client", mod, _, fix), module_file("client", p, mod).

.decl py_fixture_conftest(fix:symbol, dir:symbol)
py_fixture_conftest(fix, d) :- py_fixture_in_file(fix, p), py_fixture_scope_file(c),
    strlen(p) >= strlen(c), substr(p, strlen(p) - strlen(c), strlen(c)) = c,
    d = substr(p, 0, strlen(p) - strlen(c)).

// py_fixture_plugin(Fixture) -- a fixture of a module some module names in its
// `pytest_plugins` global. A plugin is registered for the whole session, so its fixtures
// are visible to every test, below any conftest or same-module fixture of the name.
// The value is a string, or a list or tuple of strings, each a dotted module name,
// joined to the module by that exact qualified name.
.decl py_fixture_plugin_name(q:symbol)
py_fixture_plugin_name(q) :- py_fixture_plugins_var(n), py_module_level_binding(_, n, b),
    expr_binding("client", b, "STORE", tgt), assign_pair("client", tgt, val),
    expr_node("client", "LITERAL", _, q, val), q != "".
py_fixture_plugin_name(q) :- py_fixture_plugins_var(n), py_module_level_binding(_, n, b),
    expr_binding("client", b, "STORE", tgt), assign_pair("client", tgt, val),
    expr_parent("client", val, "ELEMENT", _, el),
    expr_node("client", "LITERAL", _, q, el), q != "".
.decl py_fixture_plugin(fix:symbol)
py_fixture_plugin(fix) :- py_fixture_plugin_name(q), module_decl("client", _, q, _, mod),
    module_file("client", p, mod), py_fixture_in_file(fix, p).

// A requested parameter: no default, not a receiver, on a test or another fixture.
// And NOT an argument the test's own parametrize decorator supplies DIRECTLY: the runner
// passes that value in and never runs the fixture of the name (#1529). An `indirect`
// one is handed to the fixture, which does run, so it stays a request.
.decl py_fixture_param(req:symbol, name:symbol)
py_fixture_param(req, name) :- py_fixture_requester(req),
    param_decl("client", name, _, _, req, ph), name != "",
    param_default("client", "false", _, ph),
    param_kind("client", _, _, "false", ph).

.decl py_parametrize_on(req:symbol, h:symbol)
py_parametrize_on(req, h) :- decorator_target("client", _, req, h), req != "",
    py_deco_named(h, v), py_parametrize_deco(v).
// the argnames, as the string "a, b" (first positional, or the keyword) ...
.decl py_parametrize_argnames(h:symbol, s:symbol)
py_parametrize_argnames(h, s) :- py_parametrize_on(_, h),
    annotation_arg("client", _, s, "STRING_LITERAL", "0", "false", h, _).
py_parametrize_argnames(h, s) :- py_parametrize_on(_, h),
    annotation_arg("client", k, s, "STRING_LITERAL", _, "true", h, _),
    py_parametrize_argnames_kw(k).
.decl py_parametrize_argname(req:symbol, h:symbol, name:symbol)
py_parametrize_argname(req, h, name) :- py_fixture_param(req, name),
    py_parametrize_on(req, h), py_parametrize_argnames(h, s),
    py_argnames_sep(sep), py_argnames_lead(lead),
    contains(cat(lead, cat(name, sep)), cat(sep, cat(s, sep))).
// ... or as a list or tuple of strings.
py_parametrize_argname(req, h, name) :- py_fixture_param(req, name),
    py_parametrize_on(req, h), decorator_expr("client", de, h), call_arg(de, "0", a),
    expr_parent("client", a, "ELEMENT", _, el),
    expr_node("client", "LITERAL", _, name, el).
.decl py_parametrize_indirect(h:symbol, name:symbol)
py_parametrize_indirect(h, name) :- py_parametrize_argname(_, h, name),
    annotation_arg("client", k, v, _, _, "true", h, _),
    py_parametrize_indirect_kw(k), py_parametrize_indirect_all(v).
py_parametrize_indirect(h, name) :- py_parametrize_argname(_, h, name),
    decorator_expr("client", de, h), call_kwarg(de, k, a), py_parametrize_indirect_kw(k),
    expr_parent("client", a, "ELEMENT", _, el),
    expr_node("client", "LITERAL", _, name, el).
.decl py_parametrize_direct(req:symbol, name:symbol)
py_parametrize_direct(req, name) :- py_parametrize_argname(req, h, name),
    !py_parametrize_indirect(h, name).

.decl py_fixture_request(req:symbol, name:symbol)
py_fixture_request(req, name) :- py_fixture_param(req, name),
    !py_parametrize_direct(req, name).

// A REQUEST WRITTEN IN THE BODY (#1514). `request.getfixturevalue("admin_user")` makes
// the runner build that fixture for this test while it runs, by the same visibility
// rules as a parameter, so it is a request like one and goes through the same scoping
// below. Only in a requester's own body: a helper function calling it is not collected,
// and which test calls the helper is a call edge's question, not this rule's.
.decl py_fixture_value_call(req:symbol, e:symbol)
// SELECTIVE ATOM FIRST: there is one fixture-value method name and, on most projects,
// few or no calls to it — while there are tens of thousands of requesters. Written
// requester-first, the planner walked every requester's whole call context before the
// one-tuple filter said no: 295s to derive ZERO rows on a 526k-LOC subject. This order
// costs what the answer costs.
py_fixture_value_call(req, e) :- py_fixture_value_method(fn),
    call_decl("client", "METHOD_CALL", fn, _, e, site),
    call_context("client", _, req, _, _, site),
    py_fixture_requester(req).
// (a) the name written as a string literal;
py_fixture_request(req, name) :- py_fixture_value_call(req, e),
    call_arg(e, "0", a), expr_node("client", "LITERAL", _, name, a), name != "".
// (b) the name a parametrize decorator on the same test hands in, the commoner form:
//     `@parametrize("server", ["local_server", "remote_server"])` and then
//     `request.getfixturevalue(server)` requests each listed fixture in turn. Only for a
//     decorator with that ONE argument name, whose values are then plain strings; with
//     several names each value is a tuple, and a value that is no literal names nothing.
py_fixture_request(req, name) :- py_fixture_value_call(req, e),
    call_arg(e, "0", a), expr_node("client", "NAME_REFERENCE", _, p, a),
    py_parametrize_direct(req, p), py_parametrize_on(req, h), py_parametrize_argnames(h, p),
    decorator_expr("client", de, h), call_arg(de, "1", vs),
    expr_parent("client", vs, "ELEMENT", _, el),
    expr_node("client", "LITERAL", _, name, el), name != "".
// (c) the same with several argument names, `@parametrize("server, expected", [("local_srv",
//     200), ...])`: each value is a tuple and the name is the element at the argument's own
//     position, so only that column is read. The position is the number of separators
//     before the name in the argnames string, or its place in an argnames list.
.decl py_argnames_sep_at(h:symbol, s:symbol, i:number)
py_argnames_sep_at(h, s, i) :- py_parametrize_argnames(h, s), py_argnames_sep(sep),
    i = range(0, strlen(s)), substr(s, i, 1) = sep.
.decl py_argnames_name_at(h:symbol, name:symbol, i:number)
// A whole token: led by the string's start, a separator or a space, and followed by its
// end, a separator or a space, so `server` is not found inside `server_url`.
.decl py_argnames_found(h:symbol, name:symbol, i:number)
py_argnames_found(h, p, i) :- py_argnames_demand(h, p), py_parametrize_argnames(h, s),
    strlen(s) > strlen(p), i = range(0, strlen(s) - strlen(p) + 1), substr(s, i, strlen(p)) = p.
.decl py_argnames_led(h:symbol, name:symbol, i:number)
py_argnames_led(h, p, 0) :- py_argnames_found(h, p, 0).
py_argnames_led(h, p, i) :- py_argnames_found(h, p, i), i > 0, py_parametrize_argnames(h, s),
    py_argnames_boundary(c), substr(s, i - 1, 1) = c.
py_argnames_name_at(h, p, i) :- py_argnames_led(h, p, i), py_parametrize_argnames(h, s),
    i + strlen(p) = strlen(s).
py_argnames_name_at(h, p, i) :- py_argnames_led(h, p, i), py_parametrize_argnames(h, s),
    i + strlen(p) < strlen(s), py_argnames_boundary(c), substr(s, i + strlen(p), 1) = c.
.decl py_argnames_boundary(c:symbol)
py_argnames_boundary(",").
py_argnames_boundary(" ").
.decl py_value_row_kind(k:symbol)
py_value_row_kind("TUPLE").
py_value_row_kind("LIST").
.decl py_argnames_pos(h:symbol, name:symbol, k:number)
py_argnames_pos(h, p, k) :- py_argnames_name_at(h, p, i), py_parametrize_argnames(h, s),
    k = count : { py_argnames_sep_at(h, s, j), j < i }.
// ... or, for argnames written as a list or tuple of strings, its element position there.
py_argnames_pos(h, p, k) :- py_argnames_demand(h, p),
    decorator_expr("client", de, h), call_arg(de, "0", a),
    expr_parent("client", a, "ELEMENT", ks, el), expr_node("client", "LITERAL", _, p, el),
    k = to_number(ks).
// the argument names whose position is asked for: one a getfixturevalue call reads, and
// every argument the decorator supplies directly (its values type the parameter, below)
.decl py_argnames_demand(h:symbol, p:symbol)
py_argnames_demand(h, p) :- py_fixture_value_param(h, p).
py_argnames_demand(h, p) :- py_parametrize_direct(req, p), py_parametrize_on(req, h).
.decl py_fixture_value_param(h:symbol, p:symbol)
py_fixture_value_param(h, p) :- py_fixture_value_call(req, e),
    call_arg(e, "0", a), expr_node("client", "NAME_REFERENCE", _, p, a),
    py_parametrize_direct(req, p), py_parametrize_on(req, h), py_parametrize_argname(req, h, p).
py_fixture_request(req, name) :- py_fixture_value_call(req, e),
    call_arg(e, "0", a), expr_node("client", "NAME_REFERENCE", _, p, a),
    py_parametrize_direct(req, p), py_parametrize_on(req, h), py_argnames_pos(h, p, k),
    decorator_expr("client", de, h), call_arg(de, "1", vs),
    expr_parent("client", vs, "ELEMENT", _, tup), py_value_row_kind(rk), expr_node("client", rk, _, _, tup),
    expr_parent("client", tup, "ELEMENT", to_string(k), el),
    expr_node("client", "LITERAL", _, name, el), name != "".

// ── PARAMETRIZE AND FIXTURE PARAMS: the VALUES the runner hands an argument ──
//     @pytest.mark.parametrize("field_cls", [String, Integer])   -> field_cls is one of the classes
//     @pytest.mark.parametrize("obj, n", [(Box(), 1), pytest.param(Bag(), 2)])
//     @pytest.fixture(params=(Converter, BaseConverter))
//     def converter_cls(request): return request.param             -> one of the classes
// The runner calls the test once per row with the row's values in place of the argument,
// so each value is an argument reaching the parameter exactly as one written at a call
// site is (resolution/value-flow.dl types it). Only values WRITTEN in the decorator, or in
// a display a single-write name in this module (or one it imports the name from) holds;
// a value list built by a call is not read.
.decl py_pz_rows(h:symbol, rows:symbol)
py_pz_rows(h, a) :- py_parametrize_on(_, h), decorator_expr("client", de, h), call_arg(de, "1", a).
py_pz_rows(h, a) :- py_parametrize_on(_, h), decorator_expr("client", de, h), call_kwarg(de, k, a), py_parametrize_argvalues_kw(k).
py_pz_rows(h, a) :- py_fixture_deco_on(_, h), decorator_expr("client", de, h), call_kwarg(de, k, a), py_fixture_params_kw(k).
// the display a rows expression is: written in place, or held by a single-write name
.decl py_pz_display(rows:symbol, d:symbol)
py_pz_display(a, a) :- py_pz_rows(_, a), py_value_row_kind(k), expr_node("client", k, _, _, a).
py_pz_display(a, d) :- py_pz_rows(_, a),
    expr_binding("client", b, ctx, a), ctx != "STORE", binding_lookup("client", b, b2),
    py_pz_name_display(b2, d).
.decl py_pz_name_display(b:symbol, d:symbol)
py_pz_name_display(b, d) :- binding_rebinding("client", "1", _, _, b),
    expr_binding("client", b, "STORE", tgt), assign_pair("client", tgt, d),
    py_value_row_kind(k), expr_node("client", k, _, _, d).
// `from tests.base import ALL_FIELDS`: the module-level binding of that name in the module
py_pz_name_display(b, d) :- import_binding("client", b, i),
    import_alias("client", orig, _, i), orig != "", import_relative("client", _, pkg, i), pkg != "",
    module_decl("client", _, pkg, _, mod),
    binding_decl("client", orig, _, "MODULE_LEVEL", _, mb), binding_owner("client", mod, _, mb),
    binding_rebinding("client", "1", _, _, mb),
    expr_binding("client", mb, "STORE", tgt), assign_pair("client", tgt, d),
    py_value_row_kind(k), expr_node("client", k, _, _, d).
// a row, with `pytest.param(v, ..., id=...)` read as its positional values
.decl py_pz_row(h:symbol, row:symbol)
py_pz_row(h, r) :- py_pz_rows(h, a), py_pz_display(a, d), expr_parent("client", d, "ELEMENT", _, r).
.decl py_pz_param_call(row:symbol)
py_pz_param_call(r) :- py_pz_row(_, r), call_of_expr(r, site), call_name(site, w), py_param_row_wrapper(w).
// the value at one argument's position in a row: the whole row (one argument name), else
// the row's k-th element
.decl py_pz_single(h:symbol)
py_pz_single(h) :- py_parametrize_argnames(h, s), !py_argnames_sep_at(h, s, _).
py_pz_single(h) :- py_fixture_deco_on(_, h).
.decl py_pz_value(h:symbol, k:number, v:symbol)
py_pz_value(h, 0, r) :- py_pz_single(h), py_pz_row(h, r), !py_pz_param_call(r).
py_pz_value(h, 0, v) :- py_pz_single(h), py_pz_row(h, r), py_pz_param_call(r), call_arg(r, "0", v).
py_pz_value(h, k, v) :- !py_pz_single(h), py_pz_row(h, r), !py_pz_param_call(r),
    py_value_row_kind(rk), expr_node("client", rk, _, _, r),
    expr_parent("client", r, "ELEMENT", ks, v), k = to_number(ks).
py_pz_value(h, k, v) :- !py_pz_single(h), py_pz_row(h, r), py_pz_param_call(r),
    call_arg(r, ks, v), k = to_number(ks).
// the parameter each value reaches: a test's directly parametrized argument ...
.decl py_pz_param_value(ph:symbol, v:symbol)
py_pz_param_value(ph, v) :- py_parametrize_direct(req, name), py_parametrize_on(req, h),
    !py_pz_single(h), py_argnames_pos(h, name, k), py_pz_value(h, k, v),
    param_decl("client", name, _, _, req, ph).
py_pz_param_value(ph, v) :- py_parametrize_direct(req, name), py_parametrize_on(req, h),
    py_parametrize_argname(req, h, name), py_pz_single(h), py_pz_value(h, 0, v),
    param_decl("client", name, _, _, req, ph).
// ... and, in a fixture declared with params=, what `request.param` evaluates to
.decl py_pz_request_param(e:symbol, v:symbol)
py_pz_request_param(e, v) :- py_fixture_deco_on(f, h), py_pz_value(h, 0, v),
    py_fixture_request_param(rq), param_decl("client", rq, _, _, f, ph),
    py_request_param_attr(pa), attr_access("client", e, pa, obj), expr_names_param("client", obj, ph).

// (0) a fixture a CLASS declares serves the tests of that class and of its subclasses,
//     and nothing else; for them it wins over the module's fixture of the name. Read as a
//     plain same-module fixture it was also handed to a sibling class's tests, and the
//     module fixture it overrides was handed to its own (#1512).
.decl py_fixture_class(fix:symbol, t:symbol)
py_fixture_class(fix, t) :- py_fixture_decl(fix, _), method_owner("client", t, fix), t != "".
.decl py_fixture_in_class(req:symbol, name:symbol, fix:symbol)
py_fixture_in_class(req, name, fix) :- py_fixture_request(req, name),
    py_fixture_decl(fix, name), fix != req, py_fixture_class(fix, t),
    method_owner("client", u, req), type_self_or_ancestor("client", u, t).
.decl py_fixture_class_shadowed(req:symbol, name:symbol)
py_fixture_class_shadowed(req, name) :- py_fixture_in_class(req, name, _).

// (a) the test's own module wins next: declared at its top level, or imported into it.
.decl py_fixture_same_module(req:symbol, name:symbol, fix:symbol)
py_fixture_same_module(req, name, fix) :- py_fixture_request(req, name),
    py_fixture_decl(fix, name), fix != req, !py_fixture_class(fix, _),
    !py_fixture_class_shadowed(req, name),
    method_file("client", p, req), py_fixture_in_file(fix, p).
.decl py_fixture_shadowed(req:symbol, name:symbol)
py_fixture_shadowed(req, name) :- py_fixture_same_module(req, name, _).
py_fixture_shadowed(req, name) :- py_fixture_class_shadowed(req, name).

// (b) otherwise a conftest at or above the requester, NEAREST first. Depth is the
//     length of the governed directory, so the longest prefix is the nearest ancestor.
.decl py_fixture_conftest_cand(req:symbol, name:symbol, fix:symbol, depth:number)
py_fixture_conftest_cand(req, name, fix, dl) :- py_fixture_request(req, name),
    py_fixture_decl(fix, name), fix != req, !py_fixture_class(fix, _),
    py_fixture_conftest(fix, d), method_file("client", p, req),
    strlen(p) > strlen(d), substr(p, 0, strlen(d)) = d, dl = strlen(d).
.decl py_fixture_nearest(req:symbol, name:symbol, depth:number)
py_fixture_nearest(req, name, m) :- py_fixture_conftest_cand(req, name, _, _),
    m = max dl : { py_fixture_conftest_cand(req, name, _, dl) }.

.decl py_fixture_injection(from:symbol, to:symbol, name:symbol)
py_fixture_injection(req, fix, name) :- py_fixture_in_class(req, name, fix).
py_fixture_injection(req, fix, name) :- py_fixture_same_module(req, name, fix).
py_fixture_injection(req, fix, name) :- py_fixture_conftest_cand(req, name, fix, dl),
    !py_fixture_shadowed(req, name), py_fixture_nearest(req, name, dl).
// (c) failing both, a plugin's fixture, which every test sees.
py_fixture_injection(req, fix, name) :- py_fixture_request(req, name),
    py_fixture_decl(fix, name), fix != req, py_fixture_plugin(fix), !py_fixture_class(fix, _),
    !py_fixture_shadowed(req, name), !py_fixture_nearest(req, name, _).

.decl py_fixture_requested(m:symbol)
py_fixture_requested(m) :- py_fixture_injection(_, m, _).

framework_edge(from, to, "fixture_injection", name, "by_name") :-
    py_fixture_injection(from, to, name).

// ── 2a. THE SAME LOOKUP, FROM SYNTAX ALONE ──────────────────────────────────────
// resolution/value-flow.dl types a parameter by the fixture serving it, so it needs the
// runner's lookup INSIDE the resolution fixpoint. py_fixture_injection cannot be read
// there: a fixture a conftest star-imports is found through module_member_method, which
// is resolution, and the nearest-conftest MAX over it would then be a cyclic aggregate
// souffle refuses to stratify. This chain keeps the runner's order (class, own module,
// nearest conftest by its declaring file) and leaves out the two clauses that need
// resolution — a star-imported fixture and a pytest_plugins one. Those still reach their
// tests through the injection edge; they only go untyped.
.decl py_fixs_in_file(fix:symbol, file:symbol)
py_fixs_in_file(fix, p) :- py_fixture_decl(fix, _), method_file("client", p, fix).
.decl py_fixs_same_module(req:symbol, name:symbol, fix:symbol)
py_fixs_same_module(req, name, fix) :- py_fixture_request(req, name),
    py_fixture_decl(fix, name), fix != req, !py_fixture_class(fix, _),
    !py_fixture_class_shadowed(req, name),
    method_file("client", p, req), py_fixs_in_file(fix, p).
.decl py_fixs_conftest_cand(req:symbol, name:symbol, fix:symbol, depth:number)
py_fixs_conftest_cand(req, name, fix, dl) :- py_fixture_request(req, name),
    py_fixture_decl(fix, name), fix != req, !py_fixture_class(fix, _),
    py_fixs_in_file(fix, cp), py_fixture_scope_file(c),
    strlen(cp) >= strlen(c), substr(cp, strlen(cp) - strlen(c), strlen(c)) = c,
    d = substr(cp, 0, strlen(cp) - strlen(c)),
    method_file("client", p, req),
    strlen(p) > strlen(d), substr(p, 0, strlen(d)) = d, dl = strlen(d).
.decl py_fixs_nearest(req:symbol, name:symbol, depth:number)
py_fixs_nearest(req, name, m) :- py_fixs_conftest_cand(req, name, _, _),
    m = max dl : { py_fixs_conftest_cand(req, name, _, dl) }.
.decl py_fixs_injection(from:symbol, to:symbol, name:symbol)
py_fixs_injection(req, fix, name) :- py_fixture_in_class(req, name, fix).
py_fixs_injection(req, fix, name) :- py_fixs_same_module(req, name, fix).
py_fixs_injection(req, fix, name) :- py_fixs_conftest_cand(req, name, fix, dl),
    !py_fixture_class_shadowed(req, name), !py_fixs_same_module(req, name, _),
    py_fixs_nearest(req, name, dl).

// ── 2b. FIXTURE VALUE  (what the runner hands the parameter) ────────────────────
// py_fixture_value_type(Fixture, Type) — the value a fixture hands over: its return or
// yield value's type, or its declared return. Consumed by resolution/value-flow.dl, where
// the runner's call `test(fixture_value)` is one more argument reaching a parameter.
.decl py_fixture_value_type(fix:symbol, t:symbol)
py_fixture_value_type(fix, t) :- py_fixture_decl(fix, _),
    method_return_value_expr("client", fix, e), expr_type("client", e, t).
py_fixture_value_type(fix, t) :- py_fixture_decl(fix, _),
    method_declared_return_type("client", fix, d), declared_dispatch("client", d, t).
py_fixture_value_type(fix, t) :- py_fixture_decl(fix, _),
    expr_node("client", "YIELD", _, _, y), expr_ultimate_method("client", y, fix),
    expr_parent("client", y, "YIELD_VALUE", _, v), expr_type("client", v, t).

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 3. URL DISPATCH  (route table -> view)
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// A routing table holds the view as a VALUE. The entry is a reference, not a call, so
// the view has no caller; the framework calls it on a request.
//
// THE TABLE IS REQUIRED. `path(...)` is an ordinary name, and a call to it outside a
// route table means nothing. The table is recognised by the module-level binding the
// framework reads, which is the one thing the framework itself specifies.
// BOTH keys are carried: call_arg is keyed by the CALL EXPRESSION (the site key in
// expression-resolution IS the expression hash, call-site.dl:55) while call_context is
// keyed by the site's own unique hash. They are different values and call_decl is the
// one relation that holds the pair.
.decl py_route_entry_call(site:symbol, expr:symbol)
py_route_entry_call(site, e) :-
    call_decl("client", _, fn, _, e, site), py_route_entry_fn(fn),
    expr_in_route_table(e).

// The VALUE side of `urlpatterns = [...]`. The table name is a STORE target and the
// entries are its sibling, not its descendant, so the two halves are joined through
// assign_pair rather than by walking up from the name.
.decl py_route_root_value(val:symbol)
py_route_root_value(val) :- assign_pair("client", tgt, val),
    expr_binding("client", b, "STORE", tgt),
    binding_decl("client", n, _, _, _, b), py_route_table(n).
// The same table handed to the application as a keyword, `Starlette(routes=[...])` (#1524).
py_route_root_value(val) :- call_kwarg(_, k, val), py_route_table(k).
.decl py_route_table_value(val:symbol)
py_route_table_value(val) :- py_route_root_value(val).

// The entry expression IS that value, or sits transitively under it.
//
// SEEDED FROM THE TABLE, NOT FROM EVERY EXPRESSION. The obvious spelling is a general
// ancestor closure over expr_parent, but that materialises (expression x ancestor) for
// the WHOLE tree to answer a question about a handful of list literals. This descends
// from the table values only, so the closure is bounded by what is inside a routing
// table. (expr_parent's FIRST column is the provenance, not the parent; reading it as
// the parent makes every expression a child of "client".)
.decl expr_in_route_table(e:symbol)
expr_in_route_table(e) :- py_route_table_value(e).
expr_in_route_table(e) :- py_route_table_value(val), expr_parent("client", val, _, _, e).
expr_in_route_table(e) :- expr_in_route_table(p), expr_parent("client", p, _, _, e).

// A LIST HANDED TO include() IS A TABLE TOO (#1511). `urlpatterns = [path("x/",
// include(extra_patterns))]` mounts every entry of `extra_patterns`, and the list is
// named through a binding rather than written inline, so the descent above never
// reaches it. The name is joined to the assignment that binds it; a binding join is
// same-module, which is where such a helper list lives. `include((extra_patterns,
// "ns"))` names it one level down, as an element of the tuple.
.decl py_route_include_name(inc:symbol, e:symbol)
py_route_include_name(inc, a) :-
    expr_in_route_table(inc), call_decl("client", _, fn, _, inc, _), py_route_include_fn(fn),
    call_arg(inc, _, a).
py_route_include_name(inc, x) :-
    py_route_include_name(inc, a), expr_parent("client", a, "ELEMENT", _, x).
// py_route_mount(inc, val): the include() call `inc` mounts the table value `val`, either
// written inline (`include([path(...)])`) or named through a binding.
.decl py_route_mount(inc:symbol, val:symbol)
py_route_mount(inc, a) :-
    py_route_include_name(inc, a), expr_parent("client", a, "ELEMENT", _, x), expr_in_route_table(x),
    call_decl("client", _, fn, _, x, _), py_route_entry_fn(fn).
py_route_mount(inc, val) :-
    py_route_include_name(inc, a),
    expr_binding("client", b, ctx, a), ctx != "STORE", binding_lookup("client", b, b2),
    expr_binding("client", b2, "STORE", tgt), assign_pair("client", tgt, val).
py_route_table_value(val) :- py_route_mount(_, val).

// THE PATH A ROUTE SERVES (#1482, #1511). A table's entries are served under the prefix of
// the entry that mounts the table: `path("api/v1/", include(api_patterns))` serves
// `path("status/", views.api_status)` at `/api/v1/status/`, and a table mounted twice serves
// its entries at both. A table named like `urlpatterns` is taken as the root, served at
// `/`; one mounted by a module path string (`include("app.urls")`) is not followed, so
// its paths are those of the root and can be short of the real prefix. A pattern that is
// a regular expression is no literal path and stops the chain; a table that mounts
// itself stops at the length bound instead of growing without end.
.decl py_route_pattern_text(e:symbol, pat:symbol)
py_route_pattern_text(e, pat) :-
    py_route_entry_call(_, e), call_arg(e, "0", a), expr_node("client", "LITERAL", _, pat, a).
.decl py_route_pattern_is_regex(e:symbol)
py_route_pattern_is_regex(e) :-
    py_route_pattern_text(e, pat), py_route_regex_syntax(re), match(re, pat).
.decl py_route_pattern(e:symbol, pat:symbol)
py_route_pattern(e, pat) :- py_route_pattern_text(e, pat), !py_route_pattern_is_regex(e).
.decl py_route_entry_in(val:symbol, e:symbol)
py_route_entry_in(val, e) :-
    py_route_table_value(val), expr_parent("client", val, "ELEMENT", _, e), py_route_entry_call(_, e).
.decl py_route_prefix(val:symbol, p:symbol)
py_route_prefix(val, "/") :- py_route_root_value(val).
py_route_prefix(val2, r) :-
    py_route_prefix(val, p), strlen(p) < 200,
    py_route_entry_in(val, i),
    call_arg(i, _, inc), py_route_mount(inc, val2), py_route_join(val, i, r).
.decl py_route_served(site:symbol, path:symbol)
py_route_served(site, r) :-
    py_route_prefix(val, _), py_route_entry_in(val, e), py_route_entry_call(site, e),
    py_route_join(val, e, r).

// py_route_join(Table, Entry, Path): the entry's pattern under the table's prefix. A
// pattern written with its leading "/" (`Route("/widgets", ...)`) under a prefix that ends
// with one is joined on ONE separator, not two (#1524). A prefix is never empty (the root
// is "/"). Written with constraints rather than a negated helper, because the prefix is
// recursive through this relation and a negation inside that cycle does not stratify.
.decl py_route_join(val:symbol, e:symbol, r:symbol)
py_route_join(val, e, cat(p, pat)) :- py_route_prefix(val, p), py_route_entry_in(val, e),
    py_route_pattern(e, pat), substr(p, max(0, strlen(p) - 1), 1) != "/".
py_route_join(val, e, p) :- py_route_prefix(val, p), py_route_entry_in(val, e),
    py_route_pattern(e, pat), strlen(pat) = 0.
py_route_join(val, e, cat(p, pat)) :- py_route_prefix(val, p), py_route_entry_in(val, e),
    py_route_pattern(e, pat), strlen(pat) > 0, substr(pat, 0, 1) != "/".
py_route_join(val, e, cat(p, substr(pat, 1, strlen(pat) - 1))) :- py_route_prefix(val, p),
    py_route_entry_in(val, e), py_route_pattern(e, pat),
    substr(p, max(0, strlen(p) - 1), 1) = "/", strlen(pat) > 0, substr(pat, 0, 1) = "/".

// The view an entry names. A route registers the declaration written under any
// decorator, not what the decorator returns (#1510): `@login_required def settings`
// is served at its route, and the name `settings` denoting the decorator's wrapper is
// a fact about calls through that name, not about which def the route dispatches to.
// So an argument that names a replaced def yields the def, and only then does the
// value semantics of expr_denotes_method answer.
.decl py_route_entry_arg(site:symbol, a:symbol)
py_route_entry_arg(site, a) :- py_route_entry_call(site, e), call_arg(e, _, a).
// ... or by keyword: `WebSocketRoute("/feed", endpoint=feed)` (#1524).
py_route_entry_arg(site, a) :- py_route_entry_call(site, e),
    py_route_endpoint_kw(k), call_kwarg(e, k, a).

.decl py_route_arg_names_replaced(a:symbol, m:symbol)
py_route_arg_names_replaced(a, m) :-
    py_route_entry_arg(_, a), expr_attr_names_method("client", a, m), method_target_replaced("client", m).
py_route_arg_names_replaced(a, m) :-
    py_route_entry_arg(_, a),
    expr_binding("client", b, ctx, a), ctx != "STORE", binding_lookup("client", b, b2),
    binding_binds_replaced_method("client", b2, m).
py_route_arg_names_replaced(a, m) :-
    py_route_entry_arg(_, a),
    expr_binding("client", b, ctx, a), ctx != "STORE", binding_lookup("client", b, b2),
    import_binds_method("client", b2, m), method_target_replaced("client", m).

.decl py_route_view(site:symbol, m:symbol)
py_route_view(site, m) :- py_route_entry_arg(site, a), py_route_arg_names_replaced(a, m).
py_route_view(site, m) :-
    py_route_entry_arg(site, a), !py_route_arg_names_replaced(a, _),
    expr_denotes_method("client", a, m), m != "".
// A CLASS-BASED VIEW (#1513). `views.OrderView.as_view()` is a call into the framework,
// which returns a function that dispatches on the request method to the handler of
// that name. The class is the receiver of the call; the handlers are its methods named
// for an HTTP verb, looked up through the MRO so a handler inherited from a project
// base class counts. Only client methods: a handler the framework's own base defines
// is not a change anyone here makes.
py_route_view(site, m) :-
    py_route_entry_arg(site, a), call_decl("client", _, fn, _, a, _), py_class_view_entry_fn(fn),
    call_receiver_object(a, obj),
    ( expr_names_type("client", obj, t) ; expr_attr_names_type("client", obj, t) ),
    py_view_handler_name(n), mro_lookup("client", t, n, m),
    method_decl("client", _, _, _, _, m).

// AN ENDPOINT CLASS (#1524). `Route("/widgets", Widgets)` hands the framework the class
// itself; it instantiates it per request and calls the method named for the request's
// verb (or, for a websocket endpoint, its connect / receive / disconnect handler). The
// handlers are looked up through the MRO, client methods only, as for as_view() above.
py_route_view(site, m) :-
    py_route_entry_arg(site, a), expr_type_class_object("client", a, t),
    py_endpoint_handler_name(n), mro_lookup("client", t, n, m),
    method_decl("client", _, _, _, _, m).

// The view is whichever argument of the entry DENOTES a def. A route entry takes the
// pattern as a string and the view as a reference, so the string arguments simply do
// not denote a method and drop out without a position rule.
.decl py_url_dispatch_site(site:symbol, from:symbol, to:symbol)
py_url_dispatch_site(site, from, to) :-
    py_route_view(site, to),
    call_context("client", _, from, _, _, site), from != "".
.decl py_url_dispatch(from:symbol, to:symbol)
py_url_dispatch(from, to) :- py_url_dispatch_site(_, from, to).

entry_point(m, "url") :- py_url_dispatch(_, m).
// The edge carries the path the route serves where the table says it, so "what does this
// URL run" and "where is this view routed" read the answer off the edge. A route whose
// path is not written as a literal keeps the generic label.
framework_edge(from, to, "url_dispatch", path, "registered") :-
    py_url_dispatch_site(site, from, to), py_route_served(site, path).
framework_edge(from, to, "url_dispatch", "route_table", "registered") :-
    py_url_dispatch_site(site, from, to), !py_route_served(site, _).

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 4. SIGNAL DISPATCH  (publisher -> receiver)
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Both ends name the SIGNAL OBJECT and neither names the other. The join is therefore
// the binding the signal is held in -- the one thing both ends spell.
//
// A receiver is attached two ways, and both have to be modelled or half the receivers
// in a real project are invisible:
//   @receiver(sig)      the decorator, whose first argument names the signal
//   sig.connect(fn)     the imperative call, whose receiver names it
.decl py_signal_receiver(m:symbol, sigb:symbol)
// ── THE SIGNAL'S IDENTITY ACROSS MODULES ────────────────────────────────────
// A publisher and a receiver are rarely in one module, and joining on the local binding
// resolves only the case where they are. The obvious repair, resolving the imported name
// to a declaration the way a call does, DOES NOT WORK HERE and the reason is structural:
// a signal is a module-level INSTANCE, not a def, and the parser resolves a value-shaped
// import to its MODULE with an empty target hash. Counted on one mid-sized project's
// imports: 1225 MODULE-kind rows, every one with an empty resolvedTargetHash, against
// 817 TYPE and 708 FUNCTION rows with none empty. There is no hash to join on, so the
// join is made on the pair that IS exact -- the resolved module, and the member's own
// name -- which is the same shape resolution/name-resolution.dl uses for a member import.
//
// py_module_level_binding(ModuleHash, Name, BindingHash): a name bound at module scope,
// which is what a signal declaration is.
.decl py_module_level_binding(mod:symbol, n:symbol, b:symbol)
py_module_level_binding(mod, n, b) :- binding_decl("client", n, scope, "MODULE_LEVEL", _, b),
    n != "", scope_module("client", mod, scope).

// py_import_binds_binding(LocalBinding, RemoteBinding): `from signals import order_placed`
// binds a local name to a module-level binding in the resolved module. The member's own
// name is originalName, so an alias (`import x as y`) still joins on the right member.
.decl py_import_binds_binding(local:symbol, remote:symbol)
py_import_binds_binding(lb, rb) :-
    import_binding("client", lb, i),
    import_resolved_module("client", mod, i),
    import_alias("client", orig, _, i), orig != "",
    py_module_level_binding(mod, orig, rb), rb != lb.

// py_signal_identity(Binding, Identity): the binding itself, or the module-level binding
// it was imported from. Both ends map through this before they meet, so a publisher that
// imported the signal and a receiver declared beside it agree.
.decl py_signal_identity(b:symbol, id:symbol)
py_signal_identity(b, b) :- binding_decl("client", _, _, _, _, b).
py_signal_identity(b, r) :- py_import_binds_binding(b, r).

// BOTH ENDS ARE NORMALISED THROUGH binding_lookup. The publisher and the receiver
// each write the signal's NAME, but a name reference carries its own binding hash per
// occurrence; only the DECLARING binding is shared. Joining the raw hashes silently
// produced no edge at all while both halves looked correct on their own.
//
// py_signal_ref(Expr, SignalBinding): an expression that names a signal, in either of the
// two spellings a project writes: the bare name (`order_placed`, imported or declared
// here) or the module attribute (`signals.order_closed` after `import signals`), which is
// joined like an import, on the resolved module and the member's own name (#1515).
// Seeded from the places a signal is named, so the join never runs over every name in
// the tree.
.decl py_signal_ref_demand(e:symbol)
py_signal_ref_demand(a) :- decorator_target("client", _, m, h), m != "",
    py_deco_named(h, v), py_signal_receiver_deco(v),
    decorator_expr("client", de, h), call_arg(de, "0", a).
py_signal_ref_demand(el) :- py_signal_ref_demand(a), expr_parent("client", a, "ELEMENT", _, el).
py_signal_ref_demand(rexpr) :- call_decl("client", _, meth, _, _, site),
    py_signal_connect_method(meth), call_receiver("client", _, _, rexpr, site).
py_signal_ref_demand(rexpr) :- call_decl("client", _, meth, _, _, site),
    py_signal_send_method(meth), call_receiver("client", _, _, rexpr, site).

.decl py_signal_ref(e:symbol, sigb:symbol)
py_signal_ref(e, sigb) :- py_signal_ref_demand(e),
    expr_binding("client", b, ctx, e), ctx != "STORE",
    binding_lookup("client", b, lb), py_signal_identity(lb, sigb).
py_signal_ref(e, sigb) :- py_signal_ref_demand(e),
    expr_node("client", "ATTRIBUTE_ACCESS", _, n, e),
    expr_parent("client", e, "ATTRIBUTE_OBJECT", _, obj),
    expr_names_module("client", obj, mod), py_module_level_binding(mod, n, sigb).

// A receiver decorator takes one signal or a LIST of them: `@receiver([a, b])` attaches
// the function to each (#1515).
.decl py_signal_refs(e:symbol, sigb:symbol)
py_signal_refs(e, sigb) :- py_signal_ref(e, sigb).
py_signal_refs(e, sigb) :- py_signal_ref_demand(e),
    expr_parent("client", e, "ELEMENT", _, el), py_signal_ref(el, sigb).

py_signal_receiver(m, sigb) :-
    decorator_target("client", _, m, h), m != "",
    py_deco_named(h, v), py_signal_receiver_deco(v),
    decorator_expr("client", de, h), call_arg(de, "0", a), py_signal_refs(a, sigb).
py_signal_receiver(m, sigb) :-
    call_decl("client", _, meth, _, ce, site), py_signal_connect_method(meth),
    call_receiver("client", _, _, rexpr, site), py_signal_ref(rexpr, sigb),
    call_arg(ce, "0", a), expr_denotes_method("client", a, m), m != "".

// A receiver runs when the signal fires, whether or not this tree contains the send.
entry_point(m, "signal_receiver") :- py_signal_receiver(m, _).

// The publisher: `<sig>.send(sender=...)` (or send_robust, asend, asend_robust) on the
// same signal, named either way.
.decl py_signal_send(site:symbol, from:symbol, sigb:symbol)
py_signal_send(site, from, sigb) :-
    call_decl("client", _, meth, _, _, site), py_signal_send_method(meth),
    call_receiver("client", _, _, rexpr, site), py_signal_ref(rexpr, sigb),
    call_context("client", _, from, _, _, site), from != "".

// The two ends meet ONLY through the same binding. An ordinary `mailer.send(...)` has
// no receiver attached to that binding, so it joins nothing -- which is the whole
// reason the signal object, and not the method name, is the key.
framework_edge(from, to, "signal_dispatch", "signal", "registered") :-
    py_signal_send(_, from, sigb), py_signal_receiver(to, sigb).

// A send is joined when ANY of its identities has a receiver. An imported signal has two
// (the importing module's own binding and the declaring one), and the receiver is
// registered on the declaring one only, so a negation per identity reported a send that
// had joined as `no_receiver` (#1517). The diagnostic negates per site.
.decl py_signal_send_joined(site:symbol)
py_signal_send_joined(site) :- py_signal_send(site, _, sigb), py_signal_receiver(_, sigb).

// ── MODEL SIGNALS: sent by the library on the client's behalf (#1551) ─────────
// `post_save`, `pre_delete` and the rest are declared by the ORM, not by the client, and
// the ORM sends them from inside `save()`, `delete()` and the manager's `create()`. So the
// client has no `send` to join: the publisher is the code that saves the model, and the
// receiver is scoped to ONE model by `sender=`. The join is therefore
//   the signal's NAME     imported from outside the client, catalogued in knobs.dl with
//                         the write methods that fire it;
//   the sender's CLASS    written as `sender=Order` on the receiver, and the class of the
//                         receiver of the write (`order.save()`, `Order.objects.create()`).
// A receiver whose static class is a base of the sender may be saving that model, so the
// sender may be the write's class or a subclass of it; never an unrelated class.
.decl py_lib_signal_name(lb:symbol, sig:symbol)
py_lib_signal_name(lb, orig) :- import_binding("client", lb, i),
    import_alias("client", orig, _, i), py_model_signal_method(orig, _),
    !py_import_binds_binding(lb, _).

.decl py_model_signal_receiver(m:symbol, sig:symbol, t:symbol)
py_model_signal_receiver(m, sig, t) :-
    decorator_target("client", _, m, h), m != "",
    py_deco_named(h, v), py_signal_receiver_deco(v),
    decorator_expr("client", de, h), call_arg(de, "0", a), py_signal_refs(a, lb),
    py_lib_signal_name(lb, sig),
    py_signal_sender_kw(k), call_kwarg(de, k, s), expr_type_class_object("client", s, t).
py_model_signal_receiver(m, sig, t) :-
    call_decl("client", _, meth, _, ce, site), py_signal_connect_method(meth),
    call_receiver("client", _, _, rexpr, site), py_signal_ref(rexpr, lb),
    py_lib_signal_name(lb, sig),
    call_arg(ce, "0", a), expr_denotes_method("client", a, m), m != "",
    py_signal_sender_kw(k), call_kwarg(ce, k, s), expr_type_class_object("client", s, t).

// The write: an instance method on a receiver of a known class (`order.save()`), or the
// manager's method on the class (`Order.objects.create(...)`).
.decl py_model_write(e:symbol, from:symbol, meth:symbol, t:symbol)
py_model_write(e, from, meth, t) :-
    call_decl("client", _, meth, _, e, site), py_model_signal_method(_, meth),
    call_recv_type(e, t),
    call_context("client", _, from, _, _, site), from != "".
py_model_write(e, from, meth, t) :-
    call_decl("client", _, meth, _, e, site), py_model_signal_method(_, meth),
    call_receiver_object(e, obj),
    expr_node("client", "ATTRIBUTE_ACCESS", _, attr, obj), py_model_manager_attr(attr),
    expr_parent("client", obj, "ATTRIBUTE_OBJECT", _, cls),
    expr_type_class_object("client", cls, t),
    call_context("client", _, from, _, _, site), from != "".

framework_edge(from, to, "signal_dispatch", sig, "registered") :-
    py_model_write(_, from, meth, t), py_model_signal_method(sig, meth),
    py_model_signal_receiver(to, sig, t2), type_self_or_ancestor("client", t2, t).

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 5. DEPENDENCY-INJECTION PROVIDERS  (handler -> provider)
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// `def handler(svc = Depends(get_service))`. The marker receives the provider as a
// VALUE and the framework calls it. A provider may declare its own, so the chain has
// to carry.
//
// THE MARKER IS READ WHEREVER THE FRAMEWORK READS IT, not only as a default:
//   def h(svc = Depends(get_service))                      a parameter's default
//   def h(svc: Annotated[Service, Depends(get_service)])   Annotated metadata
//   ServiceDep = Annotated[Service, Depends(get_service)]  the same, through an alias
//   def h(svc: ServiceDep)                                 (often in another module)
//   @router.get("/", dependencies=[Depends(check)])        a route's dependency list
//   router = APIRouter(dependencies=[Depends(check)])      every route on that router
// Reading only the first made the documented spelling (Annotated) invisible.
//
// WHAT THE FRAMEWORK CALLS is decided by the marker's argument, not by its name:
//   a def            the def                         Depends(get_service)
//   a class          its constructor                 Depends(Filter)
//   nothing          the annotated class's           c: Filter = Depends()
//                    constructor                     c: Annotated[Filter, Depends()]
//   an instance      type(instance).__call__         Depends(checker)
// A class is found through expr_type_class_object and an instance through expr_type,
// so an instance resolves to ITS class's __call__, never to another __call__ that only
// shares the name.
//
// `Depends(get_service())` calls the provider eagerly: that is an ordinary call site,
// already a resolved edge, and the argument denotes the RESULT, so the def clause drops
// it. Only when that result is an instance of a class with __call__ does the instance
// clause see it, and that __call__ is exactly what the framework then calls.

// ── py_di_marker_call(MarkerExpr, Marker) ────────────────────────────────────
.decl py_di_marker_call(de:symbol, marker:symbol)
py_di_marker_call(de, marker) :-
    call_decl("client", _, marker, _, de, _), py_di_marker(marker).

// ── py_di_marker_arg(MarkerExpr, ArgExpr): the provider, by position or keyword ──
.decl py_di_marker_arg(de:symbol, a:symbol)
py_di_marker_arg(de, a) :- py_di_marker_call(de, _), call_arg(de, "0", a).
py_di_marker_arg(de, a) :- py_di_marker_call(de, _), py_di_marker_kwarg(k),
    call_kwarg(de, k, a).

// `Depends()` and `Depends(use_cache=False)` name no provider.
.decl py_di_marker_bare(de:symbol)
py_di_marker_bare(de) :- py_di_marker_call(de, _), !py_di_marker_arg(de, _).

// ── py_di_marker_target(MarkerExpr, Callable): what the framework calls ───────
.decl py_di_marker_target(de:symbol, to:symbol)
py_di_marker_target(de, to) :- py_di_marker_arg(de, a),
    expr_denotes_method("client", a, to), to != "".
py_di_marker_target(de, to) :- py_di_marker_arg(de, a),
    expr_type_class_object("client", a, t), type_constructor("client", t, to).
py_di_marker_target(de, to) :- py_di_marker_arg(de, a),
    expr_type("client", a, t), type_call_target("client", t, to).
// ... and an instance IMPORTED from the module that builds it (`from deps import quota`).
// A value-shaped import carries no type, so it is followed to the declaring module-level
// binding (py_signal_identity, section 4) and typed from the construction assigned there.
py_di_marker_target(de, to) :- py_di_marker_arg(de, a),
    expr_binding("client", b, ctx, a), ctx != "STORE",
    binding_lookup("client", b, lb), py_import_binds_binding(lb, rb),
    expr_binding("client", rb, "STORE", tgt), assign_pair("client", tgt, val),
    call_constructs_type(val, t), type_call_target("client", t, to).

// ── py_annotated_subscript(Subscript) / py_annotated_element(Subscript, Element) ──
// `Annotated[T, m1, m2]`, spelled bare or as `typing.Annotated`. The IR has two shapes:
// in an annotation the arguments sit directly under the subscript, in an assigned value
// they are wrapped in a TUPLE first.
.decl py_annotated_subscript(s:symbol)
py_annotated_subscript(s) :- expr_node("client", "SUBSCRIPT", _, _, s),
    expr_parent("client", s, "SUBSCRIPT_OBJECT", _, o),
    expr_node("client", "NAME_REFERENCE", _, n, o), py_annotated_form(n).
py_annotated_subscript(s) :- expr_node("client", "SUBSCRIPT", _, _, s),
    expr_parent("client", s, "SUBSCRIPT_OBJECT", _, o),
    expr_node("client", "ATTRIBUTE_ACCESS", _, n, o), py_annotated_form(n).
.decl py_annotated_element(s:symbol, el:symbol)
py_annotated_element(s, el) :- py_annotated_subscript(s),
    expr_parent("client", s, "SUBSCRIPT_INDEX", _, el),
    !expr_node("client", "TUPLE", _, _, el).
py_annotated_element(s, el) :- py_annotated_subscript(s),
    expr_parent("client", s, "SUBSCRIPT_INDEX", _, tup),
    expr_node("client", "TUPLE", _, _, tup),
    expr_parent("client", tup, "ELEMENT", _, el).

// ── py_di_param_annotated(ParamHash, AnnotatedSubscript) ─────────────────────
// The parameter's annotation is `Annotated[...]` itself, or a name bound to one. The
// alias is a module-level VALUE, and the parser gives a value-shaped import no target
// hash, so an imported alias is followed through py_signal_identity (resolved module
// plus the member's own name, section 4).
.decl py_di_param_annotated(ph:symbol, s:symbol)
py_di_param_annotated(ph, s) :- py_annotated_subscript(s),
    expr_root_context("client", "ANNOTATION", s),
    expr_owner("client", "METHOD_PARAMETER", ph, _, s).
py_di_param_annotated(ph, s) :-
    expr_node("client", "NAME_REFERENCE", "ANNOTATION", _, e),
    expr_owner("client", "METHOD_PARAMETER", ph, _, e),
    expr_binding("client", b, ctx, e), ctx != "STORE",
    binding_lookup("client", b, lb), py_signal_identity(lb, ab),
    expr_binding("client", ab, "STORE", tgt), assign_pair("client", tgt, s),
    py_annotated_subscript(s).

// ── py_di_dependency_list(CallExpr, MarkerExpr): `dependencies=[Depends(x), ...]` ──
.decl py_di_dependency_list(ce:symbol, de:symbol)
py_di_dependency_list(ce, de) :- py_di_dependencies_kwarg(k), call_kwarg(ce, k, lst),
    expr_parent("client", lst, "ELEMENT", _, de), py_di_marker_call(de, _).

// A router built with a dependency list, keyed on the binding it is stored into.
.decl py_di_router_dependency(rb:symbol, de:symbol)
py_di_router_dependency(rb, de) :- py_di_dependency_list(ce, de),
    assign_pair("client", tgt, ce), expr_binding("client", rb, "STORE", tgt).

.decl py_di_injection(from:symbol, to:symbol, marker:symbol)
// (a) the parameter's default value
py_di_injection(from, to, marker) :-
    param_decl("client", _, _, _, from, ph), from != "",
    param_default_expr("client", de, ph),
    py_di_marker_call(de, marker), py_di_marker_target(de, to), to != from.
// (b) Annotated metadata, written in place or through an alias
py_di_injection(from, to, marker) :-
    param_decl("client", _, _, _, from, ph), from != "",
    py_di_param_annotated(ph, s), py_annotated_element(s, de),
    py_di_marker_call(de, marker), py_di_marker_target(de, to), to != from.
// (c) `Depends()` naming no provider: the class the parameter is annotated with
py_di_injection(from, to, marker) :-
    param_decl("client", _, _, _, from, ph), from != "",
    param_default_expr("client", de, ph),
    py_di_marker_call(de, marker), py_di_marker_bare(de),
    param_declared_type("client", ph, t), type_constructor("client", t, to).
py_di_injection(from, to, marker) :-
    param_decl("client", _, _, _, from, ph), from != "",
    py_di_param_annotated(ph, s), py_annotated_element(s, de),
    py_di_marker_call(de, marker), py_di_marker_bare(de),
    py_annotated_element(s, te), te != de,
    expr_type_class_object("client", te, t), type_constructor("client", t, to).
// (d) a route decorator's dependency list guards the decorated handler
py_di_injection(from, to, marker) :-
    decorator_target("client", _, from, h), from != "",
    decorator_expr("client", dx, h), py_di_dependency_list(dx, de),
    py_di_marker_call(de, marker), py_di_marker_target(de, to), to != from.
// (e) a router's dependency list guards every handler routed through that router
py_di_injection(from, to, marker) :-
    decorator_target("client", _, from, h), from != "",
    decorator_expr("client", dx, h),
    expr_parent("client", dx, "RECEIVER", _, attr),
    expr_parent("client", attr, "ATTRIBUTE_OBJECT", _, obj),
    expr_binding("client", ob, ctx, obj), ctx != "STORE",
    binding_lookup("client", ob, lb), py_signal_identity(lb, rb),
    py_di_router_dependency(rb, de),
    py_di_marker_call(de, marker), py_di_marker_target(de, to), to != from.

// A provider in ANY dependency list runs, whether or not the guarded handlers are
// known here (an application-wide list, or one passed when a router is included).
entry_point(m, "di_provider") :- py_di_injection(_, m, _).
entry_point(m, "di_provider") :- py_di_dependency_list(_, de), py_di_marker_target(de, m).
framework_edge(from, to, "di_provider", marker, "registered") :-
    py_di_injection(from, to, marker).

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 6. ORM AND MODEL HOOKS
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// A lifecycle or validation hook is registered by decoration and called by the library.
// Most have no second end in the client -- nothing here fires the hook -- so the hook is
// an ENTRY POINT. The one client end some of them do have is the code that BUILDS the
// model, which runs its construction-time hooks; that edge is drawn below (model_hook),
// and no other caller is claimed.
//
// The registration must NAME ITS TARGET: a hook always says what it hooks (a mapper and
// an event, or the fields it validates). A project's own decorator spelled the same way
// takes no such argument, and a bare call to a function of that name is not a
// registration at all.
.decl py_orm_hook(m:symbol, tail:symbol)
py_orm_hook(m, v) :- decorator_target("client", _, m, h), m != "",
    py_deco_named(h, v), py_orm_hook_deco(v),
    decorator_text("client", _, _, argc, h), argc != "0", argc != "".

// A MODEL CLASS: one whose lineage leaves the client, through a base the engine cannot
// see (an unresolved import) or one declared in a library. A validation library's hooks
// are methods of such a class; a class with no base, or only client bases, is not one.
.decl py_type_external_lineage(t:symbol)
py_type_external_lineage(t) :- type_self_or_ancestor("client", t, a),
    type_base_unresolved("client", a, _, _).
py_type_external_lineage(t) :- type_self_or_ancestor("client", t, a),
    type_base_resolved("client", a, _, b), !type_decl("client", _, _, _, b).

// A hook written bare (`@root_validator`, `@model_serializer`) names no target, so the
// rule above does not see it. On a method of a model class the decorator is the
// registration (#1536); on anything else a bare decorator of that name is not trusted.
py_orm_hook(m, v) :- decorator_target("client", _, m, h), m != "",
    py_deco_named(h, v), py_orm_hook_deco(v),
    decorator_text("client", _, _, argc, h), ( argc = "0" ; argc = "" ),
    method_owner("client", t, m), py_type_external_lineage(t).

// A lifecycle method the library calls on every instance it builds, found by its NAME on
// a model class: `def model_post_init(self, ctx)` overrides the library's own (#1536). On
// a class that is not a model, a method of that name is an ordinary method.
.decl py_model_post_init(m:symbol, t:symbol)
py_model_post_init(m, t) :- py_model_lifecycle_method(n),
    method_decl("client", n, _, _, _, m), method_owner("client", t, m),
    py_type_external_lineage(t).

entry_point(m, "orm_hook") :- py_orm_hook(m, _).
entry_point(m, "orm_hook") :- py_model_post_init(m, _).

// ── WHAT BUILDING A MODEL RUNS (#1532) ───────────────────────────────────────
// `Widget(**data)` and `Widget.model_validate(data)` run the class's validators, its
// post-init hook and the default factories of the fields left out, and none of those has
// a call site: the library's constructor calls them. The client's side of the hop is the
// construction, so the edge runs from the code that builds the model to each of them.
//   a construction-time validator   declared on the class or an ancestor (a serializer
//                                   runs on dump, not on build, and is not reached here)
//   the post-init hook              the one the class's MRO selects
//   a default factory               `Field(default_factory=f)` or `field(default_factory=f)`
//                                   in the class body of the class or an ancestor; a
//                                   dataclass runs it from its generated __init__ the same way
.decl py_model_build(e:symbol, from:symbol, t:symbol)
py_model_build(e, from, t) :- call_constructs_type(e, t),
    call_decl("client", _, _, _, e, site),
    call_context("client", _, from, _, _, site), from != "".
py_model_build(e, from, t) :-
    call_decl("client", _, meth, _, e, site), py_model_build_method(meth),
    call_receiver_object(e, obj), expr_type_class_object("client", obj, t),
    py_type_external_lineage(t),
    call_context("client", _, from, _, _, site), from != "".

.decl py_model_build_runs(t:symbol, m:symbol, detail:symbol)
py_model_build_runs(t, m, v) :- py_model_build(_, _, t),
    type_self_or_ancestor("client", t, a), method_owner("client", a, m),
    py_orm_hook(m, v), py_model_construct_hook_deco(v).
py_model_build_runs(t, m, n) :- py_model_build(_, _, t),
    py_model_lifecycle_method(n), mro_lookup("client", t, n, m), py_model_post_init(m, _).
py_model_build_runs(t, m, k) :- py_model_build(_, _, t),
    type_self_or_ancestor("client", t, a),
    call_context("client", _, body, a, _, site), method_kind("client", "CLASS_INITIALIZER", _, body),
    call_decl("client", _, _, _, fe, site),
    py_default_factory_kw(k), call_kwarg(fe, k, f), expr_denotes_method("client", f, m), m != "".

framework_edge(from, to, "model_hook", detail, "registered") :-
    py_model_build(_, from, t), py_model_build_runs(t, to, detail), from != to.

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 6b. APPLICATION LIFECYCLE  (#1523)
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// An application registers callables it runs around every request, on an error, or at
// start and stop. None has a call site in the client, so each is an entry point:
//   @app.middleware("http")          a decorator in the py_app_hook_deco catalogue, which
//   @app.exception_handler(Exc)      must NAME what it hooks (an argument): a bare
//   @app.on_event("startup")         decorator of the same name is some object's method
//   FastAPI(lifespan=fn)             a keyword in py_app_lifecycle_kw, holding the callable
//   Starlette(on_startup=[fn])       or a list of them
//   app.add_middleware(Timing)       a middleware CLASS, registered by a catalogued method
//   Middleware(Timing)               or wrapper; the framework builds it and calls its
//                                    request handler (py_middleware_handler_name)
// A decorated def (`@asynccontextmanager async def lifespan`) is named by the def written
// under the decorator, as a route is.
.decl py_app_hook(m:symbol)
py_app_hook(m) :- decorator_target("client", _, m, h), m != "",
    py_deco_named(h, v), py_app_hook_deco(v),
    decorator_text("client", _, _, argc, h), argc != "0", argc != "".

.decl py_app_lifecycle_value(a:symbol)
py_app_lifecycle_value(a) :- py_app_lifecycle_kw(k), call_kwarg(_, k, a).
py_app_lifecycle_value(el) :- py_app_lifecycle_kw(k), call_kwarg(_, k, a),
    expr_parent("client", a, "ELEMENT", _, el).
py_app_hook(m) :- py_app_lifecycle_value(a),
    expr_binding("client", b, ctx, a), ctx != "STORE", binding_lookup("client", b, b2),
    binding_binds_replaced_method("client", b2, m).
py_app_hook(m) :- py_app_lifecycle_value(a), expr_denotes_method("client", a, m), m != "".

.decl py_middleware_class(t:symbol)
py_middleware_class(t) :- call_decl("client", _, meth, _, e, _), py_middleware_register_fn(meth),
    call_arg(e, "0", a), expr_type_class_object("client", a, t).
py_app_hook(m) :- py_middleware_class(t), py_middleware_handler_name(n),
    mro_lookup("client", t, n, m), method_decl("client", _, _, _, _, m).
py_app_hook(m) :- py_middleware_class(t), type_constructor("client", t, m),
    method_decl("client", _, _, _, _, m).

entry_point(m, "lifecycle") :- py_app_hook(m).

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 6c. MANAGEMENT COMMANDS  (#1520)
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// A command is a module in a `management/commands/` package whose class of the catalogued
// name defines the handler; the runner imports the module by its FILE NAME and calls the
// handler. `call_command("close_orders")` is the in-process way to run one, keyed on that
// same name. All three names are the framework's convention (py_command_* in knobs.dl); a
// class of that name anywhere else is an ordinary class.
.decl py_command_type(t:symbol, q:symbol)
py_command_type(t, q) :- py_command_class(cn), type_decl("client", cn, _, _, t),
    type_module("client", mod, t), module_file("client", p, mod),
    py_command_dir(d), contains(d, cat("/", p)),
    module_decl("client", _, q, _, mod).
.decl py_command_handler(t:symbol, m:symbol)
py_command_handler(t, m) :- py_command_type(t, _), py_command_handler_name(n),
    mro_lookup("client", t, n, m), method_decl("client", _, _, _, _, m).

entry_point(m, "cli") :- py_command_handler(_, m).

// `call_command("<name>")` reaches the handler of the command module named <name>: the
// module's qualified name is `<package>.management.commands.<name>`.
.decl py_command_call(site:symbol, from:symbol, name:symbol)
py_command_call(site, from, n) :- call_decl("client", _, fn, _, e, site), py_command_call_fn(fn),
    call_arg(e, "0", a), expr_node("client", "LITERAL", _, n, a), n != "",
    call_context("client", _, from, _, _, site), from != "".
framework_edge(from, m, "command_dispatch", n, "registered") :-
    py_command_call(_, from, n), py_command_type(t, q), py_command_package(pq),
    sfx = cat(".", cat(pq, n)), strlen(q) > strlen(sfx),
    substr(q, max(0, strlen(q) - strlen(sfx)), strlen(sfx)) = sfx,
    py_command_handler(t, m).
framework_edge(from, m, "command_dispatch", n, "registered") :-
    py_command_call(_, from, n), py_command_type(t, q), py_command_package(pq),
    q = cat(pq, n), py_command_handler(t, m).

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 7. DIAGNOSTICS — the half that says what did NOT join
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// A dispatch-shaped site whose receiver is not a registered task, and a receiver
// attached to a signal nothing sends. Both are reported rather than silently dropped,
// so "the engine saw it and declined" is distinguishable from "the engine never looked".
// THE SITE COLUMN IS THE CALL EXPRESSION, not the call site's own unique hash, and the
// two are different key spaces: `PY_EXPRESSION_...` against `PY_CALL_SITE_...`. The bundle
// keys call_sites.id on the EXPRESSION (expression-resolution's site key is the expression
// hash, call-site.dl:55), so emitting py_call_site's unique hash here produced a relation
// that joins to ZERO rows of call_sites and cannot be located to a file or line from the
// database at all. A diagnostic whose whole purpose is to say WHICH site was declined has
// to be joinable, so it carries the key the consumer has. call_decl holds both: column 5
// is the expression, column 6 is the site hash, and the negations below still key on the
// site hash because that is what py_task_dispatch and py_signal_send carry.
framework_unjoined(e, "task_dispatch", meth) :-
    call_decl("client", _, meth, _, e, site), py_task_dispatch_method(meth),
    !py_task_dispatch(site, _, _, _).
framework_unjoined(e, "signal_dispatch", "no_receiver") :-
    py_signal_send(site, _, _), !py_signal_send_joined(site),
    call_decl("client", _, _, _, e, site).
